<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" agent="claude-code">
  <diagram name="splint-vs-template-switch" id="splintVsTemplateSwitch">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="1020" grid="0" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="703" pageHeight="636" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />

        <!-- ================================================================
             TRUE-SIZE CANVAS. draw.io units = 1/100 inch (100 units = 1 in).
             Content occupies 0..702 units wide = 178.3 mm (cap 179.4 mm).
             Type: Arial throughout; 8.5 units = 6.1 pt (Nature 6 pt minimum).
             Palette: PLOT_PALETTE / PLOT_PALETTE_FILL slots 1..6 only.

             ENCODING, and it is the whole point of the figure:
               orange  = nucleic acid that already existed (mRNA, cDNA)
               yellow  = sequence WRITTEN by a polymerase during this step
               gray    = an oligo supplied by the protocol
               red/purple = barcode blocks, same assignment as the SPLiT-seq figure
             Panel (a) has no yellow anywhere. That absence is the contrast:
             ligation joins two ends that already exist and copies nothing.

             THREE THINGS THIS DRAWING IS CAREFUL ABOUT, each of which an
             earlier version got wrong or left out:

             1. STRAND COUNT IS CARRIED BY BRACKETS, not by the blocks. A row
                of abutting blocks reads as one molecule whatever the caption
                says, so every band brackets its molecules above and gives each
                one its own 5' and 3' terminus. Panel (a) runs two brackets in
                bands 1 and 2 and ONE in band 3, so the ligation event is
                visible as two-brackets-become-one rather than being asserted.

             2. THE POLY(A) TAIL IS DRAWN, and the oligo-dT is base-paired to
                it. Without the tail there is nothing to explain why the dT
                primer sits at that end, and therefore nothing to explain why
                the non-templated C residues land at the OTHER end. The dT15
                block and the poly(A) block occupy the same x-span on purpose.

             3. THE TSO IS ANTIPARALLEL AND OVERHANGS TO THE RIGHT. Its rGrGrG
                is at its 3' end, that 3' end pairs with the cDNA's 3'-CCC, and
                so the TSO's 5' handle must hang PAST the cDNA terminus, where
                it becomes the next stretch of template. An earlier version drew
                the TSO extending leftward under the cDNA body, which makes it
                parallel to the strand it is templating and is not a structure
                that can exist.

             Column grid:
               col A label 0..14 | blocks 30..338 | col A ends 344
               divider x=351
               col B label 358..372 | blocks 388..699
        ================================================================= -->

        <!-- ======================= HEADERS ============================== -->
        <mxCell id="hdrAL" value="a" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=10.9;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="0.0" y="0.0" width="10" height="12" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="hdrA" value="&lt;b&gt;Splint ligation:&lt;/b&gt; a ligase JOINS two ends" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=9.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="13" y="0" width="331" height="16" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="hdrBL" value="b" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=10.9;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="358.0" y="0.0" width="10" height="12" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="hdrB" value="&lt;b&gt;Template switching:&lt;/b&gt; a polymerase COPIES an oligo" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=9.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="371" y="0" width="331" height="16" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="subA" value="Rounds 2 and 3 of split-pool barcoding, and only those: barcode 1 arrives on the round-1 RT primer. A barcode is a sequence the protocol chose, so no template for it exists anywhere in the cell and it has to be delivered as a physical oligo." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=8.5;fontColor=#666666;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="0" y="20" width="344" height="34" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="subB" value="How the second PCR handle is added. After lysis the barcoded cDNA is captured on streptavidin beads and given a fresh reverse-transcriptase reaction, with the TSO supplied free in the mix." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=8.5;fontColor=#666666;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="358" y="20" width="344" height="34" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="divider" style="endArrow=none;html=1;strokeWidth=0.5;strokeColor=#CCCCCC;" edge="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint x="351" y="20" as="sourcePoint" />
            <mxPoint x="351" y="380" as="targetPoint" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>

